-- title:
--   Galois descent of Γ-stable ideals in S ⊗_W W'
-- statement:
--   Let $W \to W'$ be a commutative-ring extension with $W'$ a $W$-algebra, and let $\Gamma$ be a finite group acting on $W'$ by ring automorphisms (`MulSemiringAction`) commuting with the $W$-action on $W'$, so that the automorphisms are $W$-linear. Two hypotheses are imposed on this datum: first, every $w' \in W'$ fixed by all $\gamma \in \Gamma$ lies in the image of $\mathrm{algebraMap}\colon W \to W'$; second, there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and families $x, y \colon \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to W'$ with $\sum_{i} x_i \cdot (\gamma \bullet y_i) = 1$ for $\gamma = 1$ and $=0$ otherwise. Let $S$ be any commutative $W$-algebra and let $J'$ be an ideal of $S \otimes_W W'$ which is stable in the strong sense that, for every $\gamma \in \Gamma$, the ideal generated by the image of $J'$ under $\mathrm{id}_S \otimes \gamma$ equals $J'$. The conclusion is that $J'$ equals the ideal generated by the image of its contraction along the ring homomorphism $\mathrm{includeLeft}\colon S \to S \otimes_W W'$; that is, $J' = (J' \cap S)\,(S \otimes_W W')$ with the intersection taken along $\mathrm{includeLeft}$.
--
--   This is the ideal-descent statement for a Galois extension of commutative rings in the sense of Chase–Harrison–Rosenberg, the two hypotheses on $W'/W$ being the invariance condition and the existence of Galois descent elements $x_i, y_i$; no flatness, finiteness or local hypothesis is placed on $S$. It is used in the construction of crossing presentations after base change, where a stable ideal of a tensor product must be recognised as extended from the base factor.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ideal_eq_map_comap_includeLeft_of_forall_map_eq_of_sum_mul_smul_eq.lean

